The '''History of Guajicutea (1590–1609)''' covers the period of the [[War of the Mesogean]] between [[Burawa]] and [[Volisania]], during which [[Sarta]] executed a systematic takeover of Guajicutea through diplomacy, disaster aid, and finally military force. The war — fought primarily in the Old World — left the New World a secondary theater of naval harassment and impressment, with no direct fighting on Guajicutea. The era saw the death of [[Dawit I of Guajicutea|Boqor Dawit I]] in 1598 and the accession of his son as [[Carlos I of Guajicutea|Boqor Carlos I]], the first Guaji king to bear a Sartan name. A devastating hurricane in 1601 created an opening for Sarta, whose aid and subsequent diplomatic maneuvering — a formal alliance in 1605, the purchase of Burawa's fort in 1607, and a neutrality deal with Volisania in 1608 — isolated Guajicutea from its colonial protectors. The era ended with the [[Sarta-Guajicutea War]] (1608–1609), the deposing of Carlos I, and the establishment of the [[Sarta Colony of Guajicutea]] in 1609.

== The War of the Mesogean ==

The [[War of the Mesogean]] (1590–1610) was the first direct conflict between [[Burawa]] and [[Volisania]]. The war was fought primarily in the Old World; the New World saw little combat beyond naval impressment and the capture of merchant ships. Neither power committed significant forces to the Capuyaquiran — the war's decisive theaters were elsewhere.

In the Capuyaquiran, the war took the form of naval harassment. Burawi and Volisanian warships stopped and seized each other's merchant vessels, disrupting trade routes. Volisanian ships, seeking to avoid Burawi patrols, frequently sheltered in Guaji ports — particularly [[Barikú]] and [[Waribá]] — before continuing to the Old World with their cargo.

No fighting occurred on Guajicutea itself. Both powers were hesitant to escalate the conflict on the island, as doing so might give [[Sarta]], [[Mercia]], or [[Breisland]] a pretext to offer protection — and gain influence in the Capuyaquiran. The mutual desire to keep third powers out of the region acted as an informal brake on the war's spread to Guajicutea.

== Guajicutea during the war ==

Soldier stopovers became common as both powers used Guaji ports for resupply and shore leave. The presence of foreign soldiers was a source of tension but not open conflict — the soldiers were transiting, not occupying.

The religious landscape became more visibly split during this period. The north and east — centered on [[Waribá]] and the northern highlands — were predominantly [[Burawi Catholic Church|Burawi Catholic]], while the south and west — centered on [[Barikú]] and the southern plantation districts — were predominantly [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]]. Despite their parent nations being at war, the two churches on Guajicutea mediated peace between their communities. The churches operated with a degree of independence from their colonial sponsors, prioritizing local stability over metropolitan politics.

== Colonial changes ==

The war reduced both colonial powers' attention to Guajicutea. The [[Emissary of Burawa]] — the political representative of Burawi interests on the island — was withdrawn during this period. Only a small troop remained at the fort near [[Waribá]] to manage resupply for ships transiting to the Old world. Burawi military presence, already reduced since the civil war of 1554, drew down further as forces were needed for the war.

[[Volisania]] initially increased its presence on Guajicutea at the outbreak of war, reinforcing the fort at [[Barikú]] to secure its strategic position. However, as the war demanded more resources in the Old World, Volisania reduced its garrison, leaving only a skeleton force.

== Death of Dawit I and accession of Carlos I (1598) ==

Boqor Dawit I died in 1598, after a reign of 44 years — the longest of any Boqor. His son acceded to the throne as [[Carlos I of Guajicutea|Boqor Carlos I]], taking a Sartan name rather than a Burawi one. The choice of a Sartan regnal name marked a deliberate political shift: Dawit I had been courting Sartan trade since the 1580s, and had given his sons Sartan names to signal the island's growing alignment with Sarta.

Carlos I continued his father's policy of courting Sartan favor. He also named his own sons Sartan names, deepening the cultural and political ties between the two nations.

== The hurricane of 1601 ==

In 1601, a very large hurricane struck Guajicutea — the most devastating storm since the great hurricane of 1143 CE. The hurricane caused widespread destruction across the island, damaging settlements, ports, and agricultural land. The three cities — [[Tabakurí]], [[Waribá]], and [[Barikú]] — all suffered significant damage.

In the aftermath, [[Sarta]] provided substantial aid to Guajicutea — food, building materials, medical supplies, and personnel. The aid effort created a deep dependency and goodwill toward Sarta among the Guaji population. The hurricane and its aftermath became the turning point in Sarta's relationship with Guajicutea, transforming it from a trade partner to a benefactor.

== Growing closeness with Sarta ==

{{main|Sarta-Guajicutea alliance}}

Sarta — a nation with no colonies of its own — had been eyeing Guajicutea for years, but had sought to acquire it with the permission of [[Burawa]] and [[Volisania]] rather than by force. The War of the Mesogean, which occupied both powers, gave Sarta the opening it needed. Burawa and Volisania, unwilling to negotiate changes to the colonial arrangement while at war, effectively left Guajicutea in limbo.

In 1600, Sarta sent an emissary to [[Tabakurí]] to establish formal relations. The emissary's arrival was a diplomatic milestone — the first permanent foreign representation on Guajicutea not tied to Burawa or Volisania.

In 1605, Sarta and Guajicutea signed a formal alliance. The alliance deepened economic ties and gave Sarta a political foothold on the island. The alliance angered Burawa, but Burawa was in the middle of the War of the Mesogean and could not respond effectively. The alliance was the first step in Sarta's systematic isolation of Guajicutea from its colonial protectors.

== Sarta's diplomatic maneuvering ==

Having secured the alliance, Sarta moved to remove the remaining colonial military presences on Guajicutea.

In 1607, Sarta paid Burawa 50,000 gold coins for the fort at [[Waribá]]. Burawa, engaged in the war and in need of funds, accepted. The sale removed the last Burawi military presence on the island — only the small resupply troop remained, and it too was withdrawn after the sale. The fort was now Sartan property.

In 1608, Sarta arranged a deal with [[Volisania]]: in exchange for Sarta's neutrality in all other affairs of the War of the Mesogean, Volisania would withdraw from Guajicutea. The deal removed the Volisanian presence at [[Barikú]] and ensured that Volisania would not interfere with Sarta's plans for the island.

By mid-1608, Guajicutea was free of both Burawi and Volisanian military presence for the first time in over 70 years. But the absence of colonial protectors did not mean independence — it meant that Sarta was now the only external power with a presence on the island.

== The Sarta-Guajicutea War (1608–1609) ==

{{main|Sarta-Guajicutea War}}

In late 1608, Sarta sent a fleet to Guajicutea. The [[Sarta-Guajicutea War]] was brief — Sarta had already removed all potential opposition. Burawa's fort was in Sartan hands, Volisania had withdrawn, and the Guaji military was limited to feudal lords' personal retinues with no standing army.

The Sartan fleet captured [[Tabakurí]], the inland capital, in early 1609. [[Carlos I of Guajicutea|Boqor Carlos I]] was deposed. The king's fate — whether exile, imprisonment, or execution — remains a matter of historical dispute.

== Establishment of the Sarta Colony of Guajicutea (1609) ==

The Sarta takeover was met with widespread hostility on Guajicutea. The establishment of the [[Sarta Colony of Guajicutea]] in 1609 ended 73 years of Guaji kingship under Burawi vassalage (1536–1609) and over a millennium of Guaji self-governance dating to the unification of 516 CE.

=== Purge of the feudal lords ===

The Sartan colonial administration moved quickly to dismantle the existing power structure. All feudal [[bikabári|bikabáris]] were purged — removed from their positions and stripped of their territorial authority. The sixteen bikabás that had governed the island's territories since the feudal reforms of the 1530s were replaced with Sartan provincial administrators. The bikabá territories were converted into provinces, each managed by a regional administrator appointed by the colonial governor. The purge was thorough and swift — the Sartans brought a large bureaucracy and military to manage their first colony, and they did not intend to share power with local elites.

=== Religious reorganization ===

Both the [[Burawi Catholic Church|Burawi Catholic]] and [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]] missions on Guajicutea were expelled. The Sartans, who followed the Petran Catholic tradition, brought their own missionaries and bishops to take over the religious life of the island. The existing church establishments — tied to Burawi and Volisanian colonial authority — were replaced by a Sartan Petran Catholic hierarchy loyal to the new colonial government. The expulsion removed the last institutional ties to the previous colonial powers.

=== Economic policy and labor ===

The Sartan administration began by expanding the island's farms, aiming to increase agricultural output for export. Initially, the colonial government considered reintroducing slavery to provide labor for the expanded plantations. However, the slave trade was becoming increasingly unpopular and controversial internationally. Rather than risk the political cost, slavery was not reintroduced.

Instead, Sarta adopted a more laissez-faire approach, courting foreign diaspora communities to migrate to Guajicutea for cheap labor. The largest demographic to arrive was the [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayani]], a people persecuted in [[Balisca]] — at the time a province of colonial [[Volisania]]. The Bayani spoke Bayani and migrated in significant numbers to work in the agricultural fields. The migration diversified the island's population and provided the labor force the colonial economy needed without the political complications of slavery.

=== Language policy ===

The Sartan administration instituted strict language conformity. Most Guaji continued to speak their native language, whose writing had been adapted to the Latin alphabet during the late 1500s by Burawi missionaries. However, Sarta required all religious and trade activity to be conducted in Sartan. The policy was difficult to enforce — the Guaji population was largely rural and continued to use their native language in daily life — but it placed Sartan as the language of administration, commerce, and religion, creating a linguistic pressure that would drive dramatic changes in the island's language over the following century.

